WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.679
To begin with it's useful to understand what object orientated concepts are for your journey

2
00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:08.160
inside of Unreal Engine.

3
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.839
So we're talking about this in terms of a parent child class relationship.

4
00:00:12.839 --> 00:00:19.199
So any class can be derived from making it a parent and a class in inside of Unreal

5
00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:22.160
Engine is just a type of UObject.

6
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.960
So we see here at the bottom, all classes are the child of another class up until UObject.

7
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:35.640
So we keep almost going down another layer deeper inside of Unreal Engine before we get

8
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.700
to this UObject.

9
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:43.320
So the parent is found here, so the parent is this large blue block which in our instance

10
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.479
let's refer to this as the UObject which is the most foundational class inside of

11
00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:53.460
Unreal Engine and then maybe we'll build more classes based upon this and the reason

12
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:59.759
why we do this is because it saves a lot of having to rewrite existing functionality inside

13
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:02.100
of Unreal Engine that's already there.

14
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.459
If this base class here, this big blue block, houses a lot of foundational code,

15
00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:13.099
we can utilize that in child classes based upon that and just inherit the functionality

16
00:01:13.099 --> 00:01:18.579
and it just makes creating these projects much more streamlined, it builds a more robust

17
00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:24.739
code base as well and it just helps prototyping develop in a faster manner as well.

18
00:01:24.739 --> 00:01:30.680
So to cover what a parent and child class is, a parent sets up inherited defaults, again

19
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.780
referring to this as the foundational code base and it predefines child class default

20
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:42.120
data and functions and then the child takes on this functionality and it derives it from

21
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:48.060
an existing class and can override functionality and default properties.

22
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:53.420
So even if we set some defaults in the parent we can then override those defaults in the

23
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.099
child class and they always extend from a parent class there.

24
00:01:58.099 --> 00:02:03.400
When we're talking about inheritance inside of Unreal Engine we're talking about variables,

25
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:09.060
so variables could be a boolean or an integer or a float, it's just some variable that

26
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.900
holds some form of data.

27
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:20.460
We have functions and macros which are really great for housing some kind of code slash blueprint

28
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:28.219
scripting into almost a modular way which can be reused in other areas of the engine.

29
00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:33.580
We have components which when we're talking about working with blueprints, a component

30
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:40.419
could be a light, it could be a static mesh, it can be some form of actor inside

31
00:02:40.419 --> 00:02:46.659
of Unreal Engine that we add as almost additive functionality inside one of these classes

32
00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:47.659
that we're creating.

33
00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:52.419
And then we also have these default settings which we refer to between the parent and

34
00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:56.939
child classes in that, say you did have this light component in your blueprint so

35
00:02:56.939 --> 00:03:00.460
we created a component and now we have this light component.

36
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:04.900
Well the default setting could be the brightness of the light or the radius of the light

37
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.939
and it will come with some default settings which were inherited from the parent

38
00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.560
class, however we can override these per actor as well.

39
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:19.740
So any changes to the parent will affect the child unless the child overrides them.

40
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:25.080
So that's what's the really powerful functionality, you may have this big parent class, create

41
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.520
a bunch of child classes from this parent class and then you realise you want to actually

42
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.219
change maybe an overall variable, again coming back to this concept variable, in the parent

43
00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:41.860
class which might be the light colour and unless the child has overridden that light

44
00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:47.219
colour, if you change that light colour from blue to yellow for example, all those

45
00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:52.780
child classes will inherit the new yellow colour unless they've decided they want

46
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:53.819
to be a different colour.
